import{_ as t,o,c as n,ag as a}from"./chunks/framework.BJFNwp4n.js";const i="/images/open-source/kind/kind-architecture.svg",g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Kind Overview","description":"Kind (Kubernetes in Docker): project positioning, architecture, cluster creation flow, networking and image mechanics, limitations, and comparison with similar local tools.","frontmatter":{"description":"Kind (Kubernetes in Docker): project positioning, architecture, cluster creation flow, networking and image mechanics, limitations, and comparison with similar local tools.","date":"2026-11-16T00:00:00.000Z","recommend":2,"tag":["Kind"],"tags":["Kubernetes","Docker","Kind"],"categories":["Open Source"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/open-source/kind/introduction.md","filePath":"en/open-source/kind/introduction.md"}'),r={name:"en/open-source/kind/introduction.md"};function s(d,e,l,c,h,u){return o(),n("div",{"data-pagefind-body":!0,"data-pagefind-meta":"date:1794787200000"},[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="kind-overview" tabindex="-1">Kind Overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#kind-overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kind Overview&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Kind (Kubernetes in Docker) runs Kubernetes clusters on a single host by placing each Kubernetes node inside a Docker container. Every container runs <code>systemd</code>, <code>containerd</code>, <code>kubelet</code> and <code>kubeadm</code>; together they form a complete cluster that behaves like upstream Kubernetes.</p><p>Kind is maintained by Kubernetes SIG Testing. It began as a way to test Kubernetes itself and is now widely used for local development, CI and multi-node behaviour validation. It is a CNCF-certified conformant Kubernetes installer, but it is not a CNCF-hosted project. The former means clusters created by Kind can pass upstream conformance tests; the latter concerns governance. The two should not be conflated.</p><h2 id="design-goals" tabindex="-1">Design goals <a class="header-anchor" href="#design-goals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design goals&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Kind addresses consistency and reproducibility for local Kubernetes environments. Four design choices follow from that goal:</p><ul><li><strong>Clusters are made of containers</strong>: no virtual machines, cloud hosts or external nodes; every node is a local Docker container.</li><li><strong>Versions are fixed by images</strong>: the Kubernetes version comes from the <code>kindest/node</code> image, avoiding drift caused by host tooling.</li><li><strong>Topology is declared in configuration</strong>: node count, roles, port mappings and kubeadm parameters live in a cluster config that can be versioned with code.</li><li><strong>Lifecycles are disposable</strong>: creation and deletion are explicit commands, making it practical to provision an isolated cluster for every CI job.</li></ul><p>These choices define the boundary: Kind targets testing and development, not production.</p><h2 id="architecture" tabindex="-1">Architecture <a class="header-anchor" href="#architecture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Architecture&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Kind can be understood as four layers: the host Docker engine, the Docker network, the node containers, and the Kubernetes components and workload Pods inside each node.</p><p><img src="'+i+`" alt="Kind architecture"></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>kind</code> CLI</td><td>Management entry point. A single Go binary that creates, starts, inspects and deletes node containers through the Docker API.</td></tr><tr><td>Cluster</td><td>Cluster instance. A set of node containers sharing the same kind labels, identified by <code>--name</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Node container</td><td>Node carrier. A Docker container running <code>systemd</code> and Kubernetes components, with a control-plane or worker role.</td></tr><tr><td>Node image</td><td>Version carrier. <code>kindest/node:vX.Y.Z</code>, preloaded with a specific Kubernetes release.</td></tr><tr><td>Cluster config</td><td>Topology declaration. <code>kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4</code> YAML describing nodes, networking, port mappings and kubeadm patches.</td></tr><tr><td>Docker network</td><td>Node network. The bridge network created by Kind, assigning node IPs and carrying inter-node traffic.</td></tr><tr><td>kubeconfig</td><td>Access configuration. Host-side credentials whose context is named <code>kind-&lt;cluster&gt;</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two meanings of &quot;node&quot; need to be kept apart: a Kubernetes Node is the logical object in the cluster, while a Kind node container is the Docker container that hosts it. <code>kubectl get nodes</code> shows the former; <code>docker ps</code> shows the latter.</p><h2 id="how-it-works" tabindex="-1">How it works <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-it-works" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How it works&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="cluster-creation" tabindex="-1">Cluster creation <a class="header-anchor" href="#cluster-creation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cluster creation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When <code>kind create cluster</code> runs, Kind initialises the cluster in this order:</p><ol><li>Read the cluster config to determine node count, roles, images, networking and port mappings.</li><li>Create node containers through the Docker API and attach them to the Docker network managed by Kind.</li><li>Wait for the containers to reach a bootable state, then finish mounts, kubeconfig writes and image preloading.</li><li>Run <code>kubeadm init</code> on the first control plane, <code>kubeadm join --control-plane</code> on additional control planes, and a regular <code>kubeadm join</code> on workers.</li><li>Install the default CNI and StorageClass, then merge kubeconfig into the host configuration.</li><li>Once ready, the cluster is reached with <code>kubectl --context kind-&lt;cluster&gt;</code>. On deletion, Kind removes every node container and the network by label.</li></ol><p>In terms of responsibility, Docker supplies the node carrier, kubeadm bootstraps the cluster, and kubeconfig returns access to kubectl on the host.</p><h3 id="inside-a-node-container" tabindex="-1">Inside a node container <a class="header-anchor" href="#inside-a-node-container" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Inside a node container&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>kindest/node</code> is not an ordinary application image. It contains a node root filesystem with <code>systemd</code> boot capability, a container runtime and the Kubernetes components. Startup has four phases:</p><ol><li><strong>Prepare</strong>: the entrypoint pauses while Kind completes mounts, configuration writes and image preloading.</li><li><strong>Boot init</strong>: after the start signal, <code>systemd</code> brings up <code>containerd</code> and <code>kubelet</code>.</li><li><strong>Form the cluster</strong>: control planes run <code>kubeadm init</code> or <code>kubeadm join --control-plane</code>; workers run <code>kubeadm join</code>.</li><li><strong>Install add-ons</strong>: apply the default CNI and StorageClass, then export kubeconfig.</li></ol><p>As a result, a Kind cluster has two observation planes: Docker shows containers such as <code>kind-control-plane</code> and <code>kind-worker</code>, while Kubernetes shows the Nodes registered inside those containers.</p><h2 id="networking" tabindex="-1">Networking <a class="header-anchor" href="#networking" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Networking&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Kind networking has three layers:</p><ul><li><strong>Node network</strong>: Kind creates a Docker bridge network named <code>kind</code>. Each node container receives an IP and nodes communicate through this network.</li><li><strong>Pod network</strong>: a separate Pod CIDR, <code>10.244.0.0/16</code> by default, connected by <code>kindnetd</code>.</li><li><strong>Service network</strong>: <code>10.96.0.0/12</code> by default, with forwarding rules maintained by <code>kube-proxy</code>. Kind uses iptables mode by default; nftables is available from Kubernetes v1.31.</li></ul><p>There are three paths from the host into the cluster:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Use case</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>API server</td><td>kubectl, CI, remote debugging</td><td>Kind maps the API server to the host, so kubeconfig works directly</td></tr><tr><td>Port mapping</td><td>NodePort, Ingress, fixed-port services</td><td>Declare <code>extraPortMappings</code> to map a host port to a node container port</td></tr><tr><td>Temporary forwarding</td><td>Local development, one-off checks</td><td>Use <code>kubectl port-forward</code> without changing cluster configuration</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On macOS and Windows, Docker runs inside a virtual machine and node container IPs are not routable from the host. Workload access must therefore use port mappings or <code>port-forward</code>. Node IPs may be reachable on Linux, but port mappings remain preferable for cross-platform consistency.</p><h2 id="images-and-storage" tabindex="-1">Images and storage <a class="header-anchor" href="#images-and-storage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Images and storage&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="image-distribution" tabindex="-1">Image distribution <a class="header-anchor" href="#image-distribution" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Image distribution&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Kind nodes run their own <code>containerd</code>, isolated from the host Docker image store. Images produced by <code>docker build</code> do not appear in the nodes automatically; they must be loaded explicitly:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">docker</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> build</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -t</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> kind-demo:v1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ./app</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">kind</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> load</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> docker-image</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> kind-demo:v1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --name</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> kind-multi</span></span></code></pre></div><p>By default, <code>kind load docker-image</code> imports the image into every node, so a Pod can use the local copy wherever it is scheduled. If only some nodes are loaded, node affinity or fixed scheduling must ensure the Pod lands on a node that has the image.</p><p>Image tags should be fixed. Kubernetes defaults to <code>Always</code> for <code>:latest</code>, which bypasses the local copy and contacts a registry. Workload manifests should also set <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code> so the node prefers its local image.</p><h3 id="persistent-storage" tabindex="-1">Persistent storage <a class="header-anchor" href="#persistent-storage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Persistent storage&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Kind installs <code>local-path-provisioner</code> and creates a default StorageClass named <code>standard</code>. PersistentVolumes live on the local filesystem of a node container by default; mounting that path to a host directory with <code>extraMounts</code> writes the data to the host, so files survive a container stop and start or a container recreation. The default storage provides no cross-node sharing.</p><p>Two limitations follow:</p><ul><li>A PersistentVolume is bound to one node. A Pod using a PVC may not see the original data after being rescheduled to another node.</li><li>Deleting the cluster deletes node containers and their local data; if the storage path is mounted to a host directory with <code>extraMounts</code>, the files remain on the host, but a new cluster does not automatically recognise the old PVC.</li></ul><p>Stateless workloads should prefer <code>emptyDir</code> or no volume at all. When testing a StatefulSet, accept that data is node-local, or pin the Pod to the same node with affinity.</p><h2 id="multi-node-and-high-availability" tabindex="-1">Multi-node and high availability <a class="header-anchor" href="#multi-node-and-high-availability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Multi-node and high availability&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Kind supports multi-node and multi-control-plane topologies:</p><ul><li><strong>One control plane plus workers</strong>: the usual development and CI shape, used to validate scheduling, affinity, rolling updates and cross-node networking.</li><li><strong>Three control planes plus workers</strong>: an HA-like layout. Each control plane runs an etcd member and together they form a quorum; Kind starts an additional load balancer container to distribute API server traffic across surviving control planes.</li></ul><p>Kind removes the control-plane <code>NoSchedule</code> taint by default, so a single-node cluster can run workloads immediately. In a multi-node environment, restore the taint when workloads should run only on workers.</p><h2 id="lifecycle" tabindex="-1">Lifecycle <a class="header-anchor" href="#lifecycle" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lifecycle&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Kind keeps cluster state in Docker containers and their labels rather than a separate cluster directory on the host. The common lifecycle commands are:</p><ul><li><code>kind create cluster --name &lt;cluster&gt;</code>: create a cluster; <code>--image</code> pins the node image.</li><li><code>kind get clusters</code>: list all local clusters.</li><li><code>kind get nodes --name &lt;cluster&gt;</code>: list the node containers of a cluster.</li><li><code>kind export kubeconfig --name &lt;cluster&gt;</code>: re-export kubeconfig.</li><li><code>kind delete cluster --name &lt;cluster&gt;</code>: remove node containers, the Docker network and the matching kubeconfig entry.</li></ul><p>Kubeconfig is merged into <code>~/.kube/config</code> by default, with a context named <code>kind-&lt;cluster&gt;</code>. When several clusters exist, pass <code>--context</code> explicitly to select the intended one.</p><h2 id="limitations" tabindex="-1">Limitations <a class="header-anchor" href="#limitations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limitations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Kind targets local testing and development. The following limits come from its implementation, not from configuration:</p><ul><li><strong>Not a multi-host cluster</strong>: all nodes share one host, one kernel and one Docker resource pool, so real cross-machine failure domains cannot be modelled.</li><li><strong>No cloud-provider capabilities</strong>: there is no LoadBalancer implementation by default. A <code>type: LoadBalancer</code> Service receives no external IP unless an additional component is installed; NodePort or Ingress is the usual alternative.</li><li><strong>Storage is not shared</strong>: the default StorageClass is node-local, and data does not follow a Pod to another node.</li><li><strong>Resources are bounded by Docker</strong>: the CPU and memory limits of Docker Desktop are the limits of the whole cluster. Multi-node clusters and image builds should have at least 8 GB of memory.</li><li><strong>Not a security boundary</strong>: node containers share the host kernel; untrusted workloads should not run there.</li><li><strong>Image architecture must match</strong>: Apple Silicon builds arm64 images by default, and the node image must also be arm64. Cross-architecture builds require an explicit <code>--platform</code>.</li></ul><p>Common pitfalls and their fixes:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Pitfall</th><th>What happens</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deploy immediately after building an image</td><td>The image is absent in the nodes and Pods report <code>ErrImagePull</code></td><td>Run <code>kind load docker-image</code> first</td></tr><tr><td>Use the <code>:latest</code> tag</td><td>The default <code>Always</code> policy ignores the local image</td><td>Use a fixed tag and <code>IfNotPresent</code></td></tr><tr><td>Reach node IPs directly from macOS</td><td>Nodes sit inside a Docker VM and are not routable</td><td>Use <code>extraPortMappings</code> or <code>port-forward</code></td></tr><tr><td>Treat Kind as a production cluster</td><td>Single host, no failure domains, no cloud integration</td><td>Reserve it for development, tests and CI</td></tr><tr><td>Expect data to survive cluster deletion</td><td>Node containers and local volumes are removed; without a host mount the data disappears with the container</td><td>Mount a host directory with <code>extraMounts</code>, or use external storage</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="choosing-among-local-tools" tabindex="-1">Choosing among local tools <a class="header-anchor" href="#choosing-among-local-tools" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Choosing among local tools&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Local Kubernetes tools differ in node shape, whether they run upstream Kubernetes, and how complete their built-in components are:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Node shape</th><th>Best for</th><th>Main limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kind</td><td>One Docker container per node; multi-node supported</td><td>Testing upstream Kubernetes, CI, multi-node behaviour</td><td>No built-in Ingress, LoadBalancer or dashboard</td></tr><tr><td>Minikube</td><td>Mostly single node; some drivers support multi-node</td><td>Learning, daily development, official add-ons</td><td>Heavier startup; multi-node support depends on the driver</td></tr><tr><td>k3d</td><td>One container per node, running k3s</td><td>Lightweight multi-node environments, edge or constrained machines</td><td>Runs the k3s distribution, which differs from upstream Kubernetes</td></tr><tr><td>Docker Desktop Kubernetes</td><td>One built-in node</td><td>Docker Desktop users who want a quick trial</td><td>Single node, limited options, unsuitable for multi-node validation</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Choose by objective:</p><ul><li>Use Kind to validate upstream Kubernetes behaviour, run multi-node scheduling experiments or build CI environments.</li><li>Use Minikube for a ready-made learning environment that relies on Dashboard, Ingress and other add-ons.</li><li>Use k3d when a lighter multi-node cluster is needed and k3s differences are acceptable.</li><li>Use Docker Desktop Kubernetes for a zero-configuration trial inside Docker Desktop.</li></ul><p>Kind&#39;s value lies in matching the node shape of upstream Kubernetes and reproducing clusters from a declaration, which makes it suitable for testing node changes, taint adjustments and scheduling policies.</p><h2 id="versions-and-references" tabindex="-1">Versions and references <a class="header-anchor" href="#versions-and-references" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Versions and references&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Kind <code>v0.33.0</code> ships <code>kindest/node:v1.37.0</code> as its default node image and also supports v1.36.4, v1.35.8 and v1.34.11. Kind releases and supported node images are listed in the <a href="https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kind/releases" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">official release notes</a>; cluster configuration fields are documented in the <a href="https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/docs/user/configuration/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">official configuration guide</a>.</p>`,59)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
